<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhammadu Dikko</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1865">1865</a>–<a href="/years/1944">1944</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muhammadu Dikko was emir of Katsina in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> from 1906 to 1944. Appointed by the British after they deposed the previous emir, he founded the Sullubawa dynasty that still reigns. A supporter of colonial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indirect-rule">indirect rule</a>, he promoted Western-style education, and in 1921 he travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and made the pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammadu-dikko.jpg" alt="Muhammadu Dikko" width="320" height="521" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Muhammad Bello Kagara · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Muhammad_Dikko_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
